The '''2004 UEFA Champions League final''' was an [[association football]] match played on 26 May 2004 to decide the winner of the [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League]]. [[AS Monaco FC|AS Monaco]], a [[Monaco]]-based club representing the [[French Football Federation]], faced Portuguese side [[FC Porto|Porto]] at the [[Arena AufSchalke]] in [[Gelsenkirchen]], Germany. Porto won the match 3–0, with [[Carlos Alberto de Jesus|Carlos Alberto]], [[Deco]] and [[Dmitri Alenichev]] scoring the goals in a dominant game from Jose Mourinho's Porto. Deco was named Man of the Match.
[[File:F.C. Porto Museum 11.jpg|thumb|260x260px|The UEFA Champions League trophy is on display at the FC Porto Museum in [[Estádio do Dragão]].]]
Porto's previous triumph in the competition had been in [[1987 European Cup final|1987]] – although they had won the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] in the [[2002–03 UEFA Cup|previous season]] – while Monaco were playing in their first ever UEFA Champions League final. Both teams started their campaigns in the group stage and defeated former European champions on their way to the final: Porto beat 1968 and 1999 winners [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] while Monaco defeated nine-time champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]].

Both teams were considered [[Underdog (competition)|underdogs]] in the competition before reaching the final stages and were led by young managers. Monaco had hired former [[France national football team]] captain [[Didier Deschamps]] as manager and Porto were led by rising star [[José Mourinho]], who left the club for [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] a week after the match.

Monaco became the fourth club representing the [[Ligue 1|French league]] to reach the final after [[Stade de Reims|Reims]] in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]] & [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[AS Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]] in [[1976 European Cup final|1976]], and [[Olympique de Marseille]] in [[1991 European Cup final|1991]] & [[1993 UEFA Champions League final|1993]]. This was the fifth final in the history of the European Cup in which neither of the teams came from England, Germany, Italy or Spain and the first since the 1991 final when [[Red Star Belgrade]] of Yugoslavia beat Marseille. To date, this is the last Champions League final to feature a team from outside the [[Big Five (association football)|Big Five]] European leagues (England, France, Germany, Italy, Spain), and the last one to be won by a team from outside those five leagues.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://backpagefootball.com/fc-porto-the-big-five-and-the-death-of-european-club-competition/120080/|title=FC Porto, The Big Five and the death of European club competition|first=Richard|last=Pike|date=15 November 2018}}</ref>

==Background==
===Monaco===
Monaco finished second in the French [[Ligue 1]] the previous season, meaning that they entered the Champions League at the group stage. Monaco were placed in Group C, alongside [[Deportivo de La Coruña|Deportivo La Coruña]], [[PSV Eindhoven]] and [[AEK Athens F.C.|AEK Athens]]. After a 2–1 in their first win in the Netherlands and a 4–0 win at the [[Stade Louis II]] against AEK Athens, Monaco travelled to Spain, losing 1–0 by Deportivo. The Monegasque adventure really began after the return match against Deportivo, when Monaco won 8–3, which represented the highest number of goals in one match in the history of the new version of the UEFA Champions League; this record lasted until 22 November 2016, when [[Legia Warsaw]] lost 8–4 to [[Borussia Dortmund]]. Croatian striker [[Dado Pršo]] scored four times, while captain [[Ludovic Giuly]] (2), [[Jérôme Rothen]], [[Jaroslav Plašil]] and [[Édouard Cissé]] pulverised the Spanish defensive line. After two more draws against PSV and AEK Athens, Monaco finished at the top of Group C.

The first knockout round saw Monaco winning against [[FC Lokomotiv Moscow|Lokomotiv Moscow]] after a 2–1 defeat in Russia and a win 1–0 at Stade Louis II. In the quarter-finals, Monaco played [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]. After a 4–2 loss in [[Madrid]] (where [[Fernando Morientes]] scored, and was applauded by his former fans), Monaco created a sensation by defeating the Spanish 3–1 at home. Monaco played against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in the semi-finals, and despite the exclusion of [[Akis Zikos]], Monaco found enough strength to score twice and win the game 3–1.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/FixturesResults/Round=1715/Match=1063677/Report=RP.html |title=Ten-man Monaco dazzle Chelsea |publisher=UEFA |date=20 April 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040618003932/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/FixturesResults/Round=1715/Match=1063677/Report=RP.html |first= Matt |last=Spiro |archive-date=18 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The last goal was scored by striker [[Shabani Nonda]], who just returned from a seven-month injury. The second leg at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] saw Monaco resisting Chelsea's strikes, for a final score of 2–2 to reach the European Cup final for the first time in their history.<ref name=bbcsport>{{cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/champions_league/3743079.stm| title=Outsiders chase Euro glory |publisher= [[BBC Sport]] |date=26 May 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019}}</ref>

===Porto===
Porto, winners of the [[Primeira Liga]], [[Taça de Portugal]] and [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] in 2002–03, were the only Portuguese team in the group stage, after the elimination of [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] in the third qualifying round by Italian side [[SS Lazio|Lazio]]. Porto was drawn in Group F, along with Real Madrid, [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] and [[FK Partizan|Partizan]]. Porto's first match was at [[Partizan Stadium]] in [[Belgrade]]. [[Costinha]] scored the opening goal on 22 minutes, but [[Andrija Delibašić]] scored the equaliser on 54 minutes.<ref>{{cite news |title=Partizan seal debut point |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2003/round=1712/match=1056808/index.html |publisher=UEFA |date=16 September 2003 |access-date=17 November 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080526054939/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2003/round=1712/match=1056808/index.html |archive-date=26 May 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The next match, the first at the [[Estádio das Antas]], was a 3–1 loss to Real Madrid. Costinha scored the opening goal again, on seven minutes. [[Iván Helguera]] equalised on 28 minutes; [[Santiago Solari]] on 37 minutes and [[Zinedine Zidane]] on 67 scored Real Madrid's winning goals.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Madrid comeback floors Porto |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2003/round=1712/match=1056823/index.html |publisher=UEFA |date=1 October 2003 |access-date=17 November 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080212125631/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2003/round=1712/match=1056823/index.html |first= Graham |last=Hunter |archive-date=12 February 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Two straight wins against Marseille followed by a win at home against Partizan secured Porto's place in the first knockout round before the last match of the group stage, a draw in [[Madrid]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1712/match=1056887/Report=RP.html |title=Madrid make positive point |publisher=UEFA |date= 9 December 2003 |access-date=22 September 2019 |first=Andy |last=Hall |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040603121408/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1712/match=1056887/Report=RP.html |archive-date=3 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In the first knockout round, Porto met [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. The Portuguese won 2–1 at home<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1713/match=1059556/Report=RP.html |title=Majestic McCarthy undoes United |publisher=UEFA |date= 25 February 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |first=Fergus |last=O'Shea |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040603122433/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1713/match=1059556/Report=RP.html |archive-date=3 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> and managed to qualify in the final minutes of the second leg, when Costinha scored an equaliser in [[Association football#Duration and tie-breaking methods|injury time]] in a 1–1 draw at [[Old Trafford]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1713/match=1059564/Report=RP.html |title=Costinha turns tables on United |publisher=UEFA |date=25 February 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040603122843/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1713/match=1059564/Report=RP.html |archive-date=3 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In the quarter-finals, Porto met a French team for the second time in the tournament: a 2–0 win at home<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/FixturesResults/Round=1714/Match=1062123/Report=RP.html |title=Porto press on towards last four |publisher=UEFA |date=23 March 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |first=Fergus |last=O'Shea |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040614220821/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/FixturesResults/Round=1714/Match=1062123/Report=RP.html |archive-date=14 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> and a 2–2 draw in France eliminated [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]] from the competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1714/match=1062127/Report=RP.html |title=Away draw sees Porto progress |publisher=UEFA |date=7 April 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |first=Pete |last=Sanderson |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040605194437/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1714/match=1062127/Report=RP.html |archive-date=5 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In the semi-finals, Porto played Deportivo La Coruña, eliminating them 1–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1715/match=1063678/Report=RP.html |title=Porto denied by ten-man Depor |publisher=UEFA |date=21 April 2004 |access-date=22 September 2019 |first=Fergus |last=O'Shea |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040603125321/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/UCL/fixturesresults/round=1715/match=1063678/Report=RP.html |archive-date=3 June 2004 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

